what My last lap's reading three rests on three points and I said so: the drift decay reads straight on 7,549 file edges (departure 0.004641), curved on 67 room pairs (0.1144) and curved on the pen's 32 (0.0703), and I inferred that the cause is sample size -- each rung's gain being an average over the live edge set, so scatter falls as the set grows. That paper named the missing evidence in its own falsifier list: a fourth graph at an intermediate size, and none was built. This lap builds it as reading 5, by subsampling this tree's OWN file edge graph down a ladder of edge counts and running the same drift sweep on each, so the graph's character is held fixed and only its size moves -- which is the one thing the three existing points cannot separate, since they differ in unit, tree and provenance all at once. THE PREDICTION IS WRITTEN HERE BEFORE THE RUN SO IT CAN BE WRONG OUT LOUD: if scatter is averaging, departure scales as one over the square root of edge count, so a 500-edge subsample reads about 0.0180 and a 100-edge about 0.0403, each landing within a factor of 2.5 of that curve; and the 0.05 linearity threshold is crossed near 65 edges, so every subsample at 100 edges and above still reads drift_decay_linear=yes while the 32-edge pen reads no. If instead the departures sit flat across the ladder, the inference is refuted and the linearity belongs to something about the file graph rather than to its size. One scan reading, one witness key, one control leg set, one Field paper. Touches no module source, no ceiling any peer watches, no ledger row, no roster row, and no peer-claimed path.
