what My last lap's card asked whether the writer should offer a cut that takes a record whole, after a phantom record stood 83 minutes on the board. Reading the two windows rather than trusting that sentence turns the question over: the cut was a hand truncating a what line at an unanchored literal, and tools/f/fleet_claim.sh already has --close, which takes a record whole and would have done it. What has NO wall is the writer itself. Proven on metal in a throwaway pen before claiming, not reasoned: --what carrying a newline whose continuation begins with a field key writes that key straight into the record, and the form scan answers repeated_field twice, corrupting=2, verdict=malformed -- a claim whose seat line says a seat other than the writer's own. That is strictly worse than the phantom class the card named, because a CORRUPTING finding makes fleet_claim_scan.sh REFUSE, so one stray newline in one paste reds the pre-build reading of every ship on the pier. The writer validates a name as kebab-case and validates nothing else it writes; a what sentence is a long pasted paragraph and this fleet's own run to 1198 bytes, so a carried newline is an ordinary accident rather than an exotic one. THE PREDICTION IS WRITTEN HERE BEFORE THE BUILD SO IT CAN BE WRONG OUT LOUD: no revision of the live board in its whole history carries a corrupting finding this writer authored, so this is prevention with a proof that it moves nothing today, and if instead a revision shows the writer's own hand in a corrupting finding the lap books a red. The mechanism is two readings rather than one rule: refuse a newline in --what or --paths by name, and run the form scan on the staged tmp before the bytes land, refusing only a corrupting finding the INHERITED board did not already carry -- so a hand may still claim on a board a peer damaged, which is reported out loud rather than refused. Two files: the writer and its control. Touches no module source, no ceiling any peer watches, no ledger row, no roster row, and no peer-claimed path -- the check read overlap_peer=0 over both.
